<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaja of Opobo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1821–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Jaja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaja of Opobo was a merchant king in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a> and sold as a boy into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Bonny, he rose to head the Anna Pepple trading house and in 1869 founded the Kingdom of Opobo, which took control of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> trade. He resisted British commercial control until the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> deported him in 1887; he died in 1891 while returning from exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo.jpg" alt="Jaja of Opobo" width="320" height="571" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaja_of_Opobo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jaja's full name was Jubo Jubogha. He was of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> birth, from Amaigbo in present-day Imo State, and was later ritually adopted into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijaw-people">Ijaw people</a>. In his youth he was kidnapped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a>, possibly by the Aro Confederacy, sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and brought to Bonny. He earned his way out of slavery after serving his master for a number of years, and at his master's death he took charge of the trade and went on to head the Anna Pepple House, a merchant faction of Bonny.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Jaja the Anna Pepple House absorbed other trading houses, until a war with the Manilla Pepple House led by Oko Jumbo drove him to break away and found Opobo, 26 miles east of Bonny. As its first king, or amanyanabo, he barred European and African middlemen and so held the trade in his own hands. By 1870 he was selling eight thousand tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> directly to the British, and Opobo also shipped palm oil straight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>. He sent his children to schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, hired white staff for the secular school he built in Opobo, and kept missionaries out of the kingdom.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884 the European powers designated Opobo as British territory. When Jaja refused to stop taxing British traders, the vice consul Henry Hamilton Johnston invited him to negotiations in 1887 and had him seized on arrival aboard a British vessel. He was tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and exiled, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and later to Saint Vincent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, where his presence was said to have stirred unrest among people of African descent angered at the treatment of a king from their homeland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Allowed to return to Opobo in 1891, Jaja died on the way, and after his exile and death the power of the Opobo state declined quickly. The King Jaja of Opobo Memorial was put up in the centre of Opobo town in 1903. His son Prince Waribo, sent to Manor House School in Frodsham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, died there of inflammation of the lungs on 21 April 1882, aged 13 or 14, and is buried in the churchyard of St Laurence's Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jaja of Opobo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1869 the former slave and house head <b>Jaja</b> left Bonny to found the Kingdom of Opobo, taking much of its trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jaja</b>, born in Igboland and sold as a boy to Bonny, rose to head the Anna Pepple trading house of the Bonny Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-opobo">Kingdom of Opobo</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1887 consul Harry Johnston deported <b>King Jaja</b> of Opobo, and from 1891 Claude MacDonald, as commissioner and consul-general, set up a proper administration at Old Calabar with customs and courts.</blockquote><a href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
